# The ladder (section 7): rung 0 (no NOT gate) against rung 1 and the many-NOT files. README items: T6, C4, C4w, Theorem.
# Rung 0: Wegener and Mehlhorn. The count is tight or positive and gates >= base.
corpus M = research/one-not/data/witnesses/*.json where nots <= 0
claim forall c in M: correct(c) by lean
claim forall c in M: slack(c) >= 0 by lean
claim forall c in M: gates(c) >= base(c)
count c in M: gates(c) = base(c)
count c in M: slack(c) = 0

# Many NOT gates: the any-gate witness count falls below base (C4w, refuted), the gate count does not (C4g, open).
corpus N = research/one-not/data/witnesses/nonlocal/general_count_below_base_*.json
claim forall c in N: correct(c)
count c in N: nots(c) >= 4
count c in N: witness(c) < base(c)
count c in N: gates(c) >= base(c)
claim forall c in N: gates(c) >= base(c)
claim forall c in N: margin["C4 count (flip classes)"](c) >= 0     # the repaired family holds on all of them
claim forall c in N: margin["Theorem: gates >= base"](c) >= 0

# The smallest one, in the kernel: 4 NOT gates, witness count 5 < base 6, and still 10 >= 6 gates.
let x = load research/one-not/data/witnesses/nonlocal/general_count_below_base_min.json
claim correct(x) by lean
claim witness(x) < base(x) by lean
claim gates(x) >= base(x)
# WISH: claim forall c in N: not (witness(c) >= base(c))   (a negated claim over a corpus, instead of two counts)
# WISH: a Lean form for gates(c) >= base(c) (the Theorem has no kernel form; slack >= 0 is the OR-only count)
